tenant or  woman his daughter in the same bed in  some state of disarray  however this witness was not cold the  reason being that in the military code  of justice at that time the prosecutor  the judge advocate as he was called in  those days had total control of what  witnesses the defense was allowed to  summons and this particular judge  advocate who was clearly very ambitious  and zealous prosecutor denied the  defense the opportunity of calling the  soldier whose  testimony certainly would have  substantiated  Captain Geddes accusation there were  other witnesses who had seen lieutenant  or lemon fondle the breasts of his  daughter and kiss her passionately take  him on take her on his lap and fondle  her and in a manner that was not  appropriate for a father and daughter so  you also have to ask yourself why  Captain Geddes would have made up such  an appalling story if whatever his  motives might have been in fact when the  Judge Advocate General the Army's the  highest military judicial officer  reviewed the transcript of the trial  incident we were talking about six  hundred handwritten pages of the trial  record plus exhibits the Judge Advocate  General reviewed this very thoroughly  and said that he believed that the  Orleans were guilty he also found many  prejudicial errors of procedure in the  trial and throughout the verdict it's at  this point that General of the army  William Tecumseh Sherman gets interested  in the proceedings and his position  first of all his general position was  that military justice had to be harder  than civilian justice that civilian  justice was often soft it let people off  too easily the military because it was a  code applying to warriors should not do  this and in the Geddes case he made it  clear what he was willing to do to find  someone to find the accused guilty when  the verdict was thrown out and I must  say that the Judge Advocate General  prepared a very lengthy and I think  compelling document but it's not clear  the Chairman even read it he rushed  ahead to find a way to try get us again  on other charges to get  out of the army he didn't even consider  for a moment that or woman might be  guilty he said we've got to remove this  monster captain get us from the Army  here too I'd like to mention that  captain Geddes had an excellent field  record he was thought of very highly by  fellow officers who signed a petition  supporting him during the original trial  however he had a weakness that enabled  the army to pursue him he had been a  womanizer he had been a careful  womanizer his targets had been the  dissatisfied wives of other officers and  I think the place where he made a  serious error was that one of these  wives was the wife of the commander of  Fort Stockton and apparently this was so  scandalous that she gave birth to a  child fathered by Captain Geddes her  husband just owned her but did not  divorce her he simply sent her back to  her relatives this is what you did at  the time under such circumstances but  that commanding officer general Napoleon  Bonaparte McLaughlin was very highly  regarded in the department of Texas the  army the frontier army at that time was  a very small group everybody knew  everyone else and you can imagine that  this episode was a real scandal and that  people many people who liked mcLaughlin  were not happy about Geddes role in it  in any case  General Sherman dispatched an assistant  inspector general to go to Texas and dig  up dirt on get us so that he could be  tried on other charges there are a  couple of ironies about this as I read  the case first of all Sherman makes a  big point in his correspondence on the  trial about how the proper gentleman  must protect the reputation  of ladies it's up to him to protect  these ladies nevertheless if Sherman had  gone ahead to a trial of get us on the  kinds of charges he was digging up  evidence on the reputation of a number  of ladies would have been severely  damaged and yet it didn't matter to him  in fact I see this trial in terms of men  sacrificing women to their own interests  for example lieutenant or woman had been  exonerated by the army even before the  trial he really didn't have to have this  court-martial to get the Army's approval  so he dragged his daughter through this  very painful procedure in which she had  to submit to repeated gynecological  examinations as well as testified to  this all-male tribunal simply so that he  could emerge victorious in any case  before Sherman completed his plans to  retry get us plans which incidentally  the Judge Advocate General tried to  dissuade him from because a lot of it  was simply gossip and hearsay rather  than hard and fast evidence that would  stand up in a court it was possible to  try get us on other charges and get him  out of the army he was brought up on a  charge of drunk on duty it's very clear  from the transcript of that trial that  he was taken sick while he was at parade  he immediately turned over the his  duties to another officer and left the  parade grand a doctor confirmed that he  was genuinely sick but it was enough for  the army to get rid of him and clearly  was a real kangaroo court he was  dismissed his military service his  career was over much to his distress I  think this trial also ruined the life of  Lily or woman  did not marry until she was over 60 when  she married a widower had a brief  marriage until his death the only person  who came out on top was the man who  committed incest lieutenant or woman  retired from active duty but had a long  career afterwards as an instructor in a  military academy live to the ripe old  age of 94 now there's a little addendum  to this story that I at least find  interesting when my book was finished  but not yet  out it was listed on amazon.com is  forthcoming and because of this I  received an email from a woman who  identified herself as a  great-granddaughter of this lieutenant  or woman and she had been going to check  check out books on amazon.com to see the  listing for her own book which happens  to be an incest survivors memoir she had  known nothing about this earlier  instance in the family it had clearly  been a skeleton in the closet that no  one had talked about but she was simply  bowled over to find out that in an  earlier generation someone had suffered  the same thing that she had suffered and  needless to say I was rather chilled to  find out that the same behavior had  occurred in a later generation of the  family well I've talked a while maybe  someone has a question that they can ask  about this is the thing is like a soap  opera it's a very complex trial with all  kinds of issues having to do with women  and race comes into it too because these  were black troops who were officers by  whites stationed at Fort Stockton  I'd like to ask um how did you first  become interested in the subject well  this is a good question since I've been  identified as an English professor I  always have to explain what I'm doing  writing history I worked in my field of  specialization American literature and  culture for 20-some years and I think  probably at the time I became interested  in history I was in need of a change  something related but at the same time  different and what got me started was a  visit to the Little Bighorn Battlefield  that was a turning point I decided to  write about George Armstrong Custer and  I just plunged into the subject at the  battlefield archive and elsewhere and  wrote a book called touched by fire the  life death and mythic afterlife of  George Armstrong Custer which  incidentally won a prize the year it was  published as the best book in its field  that the Little Bighorn Associates gives  in that that area I'm very pleased with  that book in connection with my custard  work I had to look at some army  courts-martial involving Custer and  officers in his regiment and I found  these trials to be fascinating they  weren't on particularly sensationalistic  topics but just the whole mechanism of  the trial the inherent drama in any  trial got me hooked on the subject so I  started reading around in army  courts-martial and just like the  civilian justice system the military has  to deal with all kinds of issues not  simply things like desertion going AWOL  drunkenness on-duty although those are  very popular charges but murder rape  every every possible crime that people  are capable of committing  we'll turn up in military trials just as  they do in the civilian judicial system  so I found these trials very engrossing  and at a certain point another  researcher said to me well maybe you'd  like to read the trial of Andrew Gatos  so I got this heavy book 600 handwritten  pages opened it up started reading and  literally could not put it down i I just  remain glued to that text in the  National Archives reading room it seemed  as if one one thing was more fascinating  than another and I'll tell you one of  the most amazing and interesting aspects  of it was all of the testimony on  virginity again keeping in mind that at  this time people did not talk about such  things there they didn't talk about  anything having to do with sex or the  body much less incest but it was  necessary to explore the issue of  whether this 18 year old girl had indeed  had sexual experience too to talk about  her virginity one doctor examined her  and for me the chilling thing about his  testimony is that it's full of  inaccuracy the reason being that at that  time women did not routinely have  gynecological examinations they only had  examinations in connection with  childbirth or some terrible problem it  just wasn't done so most doctors had  very little experience with a a virginal  woman or a woman who had sexual  experience but had not become pregnant  I'm not going to go into all of these  gory details I was sitting there reading  sometimes finding myself just completely  repulsed by what I was reading but for  example this doctor testified that of  course Lily or woman was a virgin  because  her nipples were still virginal well I'm  sure most of you know that the changes  that take place in woman's breasts occur  as a result of pregnancy and then more  as a result of childbirth but they do  not change the result of just plain  sexual experience the doctor also said  that his examination of lily hurt Lily's  genitals indicated that she had not had  sexual experience well he simply tried  to examine her manually and I'm sure she  had been coached to say of you're  hurting me please stop the minute he  approached the area and being a good  Victorian gentleman he wouldn't have  tried to pursue this then the defense  because the prosecution and testified  that Lily had not had sexual experience  they had to counter this by bringing in  like a half dozen doctors all of whom  talked about virginity and here the part  that's interesting is that this all-male  Court which in general in court-martial  and the court-martial a procedure the  jury the members of the court don't ask  a lot of questions they leave that up to  the judge advocate well suddenly this  court comes alive and everybody is  asking questions about virginity even  though a lot of the questions had very  little to do with the actual case it's  as if suddenly it was acceptable within  this framework to to find out anything  that they could about this important  issue so that's that gives us I think a  very interesting picture of how people  thought about sexuality at the time and  about women and incidentally although  this is the military trial it seems to  me what we're dealing with is simply the  way society was at the time it would  have been very similar in terms of the  emotions aroused and the attitude  express'd if it had been a civilian  trial what makes it a particularly  military of course in addition to the  fact that these men were officers is the  procedure of the military court I've  already mentioned that the Judge  Advocate could veto defense witnesses  which could be a terrible handicap  obviously to mounting a vigorous defense  in addition something that strikes me  about military procedure at the time in  trials as being very significant is the  fact that the judge advocate the  prosecutor deliberate with the jury with  the court now think of the tremendous  advantage that is you know he gets to he  doesn't even have to give a closing  statement because he's going to be  meeting with them and if they have any  questions he'll answer them according to  his point of view which is the site of  of finding captain Geddes guilty so  incidentally in case you're wondering  the system has been reformed since that  time the military the uniformed military  code of the military code uniform  justice in 1950 has changed that so that  those practices that would seem to us  particularly unfair today no longer  exist in the the court-martial procedure  than quite often because of the court  procedures at that time do you think  cases like that happened often that  innocent people were convicted or you  know similar cases to this and they just  were kept secret it's hard to say um  when you read a trial transcript you're  only getting the tip of the iceberg very  often you know all you have is the court  record and often you can't get further  information about what the real story is  for example nothing about major  McLaughlin wife and her affair with  Captain Geddes comes out in the trial I  was able to get that from other sources  but often you don't have this kind of  secret information I think probably most  courts-martial were fair for example  people who deserted and were captured  and brought back and tried for desertion  it's pretty open-and-shut and I've read  a lot of these cases and by and large  they're fair but this was an exceptional  situation first of all you know the  subject was unheard of and I would say  that any institution whether it's the  military or a large corporation or some  other branch of government does not want  unfavorable publicity the army had a  stake in keeping this accusation down in  in finding in choosing between Geddes  and Orel Minh you were choosing between  a pretty standard kind of charge namely  here's a young officer who's trying to  seduce a young woman that's you know not  right but it's nothing to to cause  people to get too surprised by on the  other hand the idea that a man is  committing incest with his daughter an  officer of the army clearly this is  ungentlemanly behavior that would have  been very bad publicity  for the army so I can really understand  the institutional desire to keep down  this publicity and ultimately to make a  choice between the career of this one  man and the greater good of the entire  organization however I don't see that  choice really being made it seems to me  it's more insidious or more subtle than  that it seems to me that everybody from  Sherman on down in the military  hierarchy was completely convinced that  Andrew Geddes was lying about lieutenant  or woman and to me what this says is  that at that time in our society people  just could not entertain the idea of  something as horrible as incest they  wanted to believe it could never happen  it didn't happen that anyone who talked  about it was some sort of degenerate  himself otherwise he wouldn't be talking  about it and that that was the attitude  that ultimately did in Captain get us  when he tried to be a good Samaritan and  rescue this young woman of course I  haven't told you all of the details that  are in the book but there is so much  that substantiates the guilt of  lieutenant or woman all of the  information that get us said that Lily  had told him it's just the kind of stuff  you can't imagine he would make up that  having to do with for example her aunt  from Germany had come and discovered  that this was happening  unless Lily had told him that there's no  way that captain get us could